<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Design Tools</span>
      <h1>Figma vs Sketch: Which Design Tool Is Better in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      Figma and Sketch are the two design tools most often shortlisted for product and UI design work in 2026, and they are not interchangeable. Figma wins on browser-based collaboration, cross-platform support, and real-time multiplayer editing. Sketch wins on native Mac performance, a lower price for solo designers, and a mature plugin ecosystem. This comparison breaks down collaboration, prototyping, components, pricing, platform support, and ecosystem so you can pick the right one for your team.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://www.figma.com" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Figma Free</a>
      <a href="https://www.sketch.com" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Sketch Free</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Figma</th><th>Sketch</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Teams, collaboration, cross-platform</td><td>Solo Mac designers, native performance</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Platform</td><td>Web, macOS, Windows</td><td>macOS only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Real-time Collaboration</td><td>Yes, multiplayer</td><td>Yes, via Sketch Cloud (slower)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free Tier</td><td>Yes, 3 projects</td><td>Yes, 30-day trial</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Professional Price</td><td>$12/editor/mo</td><td>$10/editor/mo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Prototyping</td><td>Advanced with variables</td><td>Basic, improving</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Components</td><td>Variants, properties, variants</td><td>Symbols (mature)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dev Mode</td><td>Yes, included</td><td>Yes, via Sketch Cloud</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plugin Ecosystem</td><td>Growing, official since 2019</td><td>Larger, more mature</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Offline Editing</td><td>Limited (desktop app)</td><td>Yes, native</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Auto Layout</td><td>Yes, advanced</td><td>Yes, via Smart Layout</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Variables and Tokens</td><td>Yes, full support</td><td>Yes, via variables</td></tr>
        <tr><td>File Format</td><td>.fig (binary)</td><td>.sketch (JSON-based)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile App</td><td>iOS, Android (view only)</td><td>iOS (view via Sketch Cloud)</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Figma?</h2>
    <p>Figma is a browser-based design tool founded in 2012 by Dylan Field and Evan Wallace. It launched publicly in 2016 and has grown to over 4 million users as of 2026, making it the most widely used UI design tool in the world. Figma's defining feature is real-time multiplayer collaboration, where multiple designers can edit the same file simultaneously, similar to Google Docs.</p>
    <p>The current version supports design, prototyping, Dev Mode for developer handoff, FigJam for whiteboarding, and a growing set of AI features. Figma runs in the browser on any operating system, with native desktop apps for macOS and Windows that wrap the web experience. Pricing starts at free for up to 3 projects, Professional at $12 per editor per month, and Organization at $45 per editor per month. Figma was acquired by Adobe in 2023 but continues to operate as an independent product.</p>

    <h2>What Is Sketch?</h2>
    <p>Sketch is a Mac-only vector design tool founded in 2010 by Pieter Omvlee. It launched as an alternative to Adobe Illustrator and Photoshop, focused specifically on UI and web design rather than print or illustration. Sketch pioneered the modern UI design workflow with symbols, shared styles, and export presets, and it was the dominant UI design tool from 2014 to 2018 before Figma overtook it.</p>
    <p>The current version supports design, prototyping, collaboration via Sketch Cloud, and a mature plugin ecosystem. Sketch is Mac-only, which limits its use in cross-platform teams, but it offers native performance that browser-based tools cannot match. Pricing starts at $10 per editor per month or $120 per year, with a Mac license option. Sketch remains the choice of many solo Mac designers and agencies that standardize on Apple hardware.</p>

    <h2>Collaboration and Multiplayer Editing</h2>
    <p>How a design tool handles collaboration defines the modern design workflow. Figma is the clear leader here.</p>

    <h3>Figma Multiplayer</h3>
    <p>Figma's real-time multiplayer editing is the best in the category. Multiple designers can edit the same file simultaneously, with live cursors, selection highlights, and voice chat built in. Comments and annotations are attached to specific frames, and version history is automatic. Figma's collaboration model is so seamless that it has become the default for remote design teams. Non-designers like product managers and developers can view files, leave comments, and inspect designs without a license.</p>

    <h3>Sketch Collaboration</h3>
    <p>Sketch added collaboration through Sketch Cloud, where teams can share documents and edit together. The experience is functional but slower than Figma, with less fluid real-time editing. Sketch Cloud works best for async collaboration, where designers upload work and reviewers leave comments. For live co-editing, Figma is clearly better. Sketch also requires a Mac for editing, which means Windows-based teammates cannot contribute to design files.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Figma</span> wins collaboration and multiplayer editing.</p>

    <h2>Platform Support and Cross-Platform</h2>
    <p>Where the tool runs matters for team composition. Figma is cross-platform, while Sketch is Mac-only.</p>

    <h3>Figma Platform</h3>
    <p>Figma runs in any modern browser on macOS, Windows, Linux, or ChromeOS. Native desktop apps for macOS and Windows provide a slightly better experience with offline caching, but the browser version is fully functional. This means any teammate, regardless of operating system, can view, edit, and contribute to design files. For teams with mixed hardware, Figma is the only practical choice.</p>

    <h3>Sketch Platform</h3>
    <p>Sketch is macOS only. There is no native Windows or Linux app, and no web editor. Sketch Cloud allows web-based viewing and commenting, but actual design work requires a Mac. This is the single biggest reason teams switch from Sketch to Figma. For solo Mac designers or all-Mac agencies, Sketch is fine, but for any team with Windows or Linux users, Sketch is a non-starter.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Figma</span> wins platform support.</p>

    <h2>Prototyping and Interaction Design</h2>
    <p>Prototyping lets designers create interactive previews of their designs. Figma has the stronger prototyping toolset.</p>

    <h3>Figma Prototyping</h3>
    <p>Figma's Prototype mode supports transitions, smart animate, interactive components with variants, variables for dynamic content, and conditional logic. You can build realistic prototypes that respond to user input, display different states, and animate between screens. Figma prototypes can be shared via a link and viewed on any device, including mobile. For product designers who need to validate flows before development, Figma is the most capable browser-based prototyping tool.</p>

    <h3>Sketch Prototyping</h3>
    <p>Sketch has a native Prototype feature that supports hotspots, transitions, and overlays. It is functional for basic click-through prototypes but lacks variables, conditional logic, and advanced animation. Sketch prototypes can be shared via Sketch Cloud and viewed in a browser or on iOS. For simple prototypes, Sketch is sufficient, but for complex interactive prototypes, Figma is clearly better.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Figma</span> wins prototyping and interaction design.</p>

    <h2>Components and Design Systems</h2>
    <p>Components let designers reuse elements across files. Both tools have mature component systems, but they differ in approach.</p>

    <h3>Figma Components</h3>
    <p>Figma's component system supports variants, component properties, boolean operations, and nested instances. The Variables feature lets you define color, number, string, and boolean variables that can be applied across a design system, which is essential for theming and dark mode. Figma also supports design tokens via plugins, and the Team Library lets you share components across projects and teams.</p>

    <h3>Sketch Symbols</h3>
    <p>Sketch's symbol system is mature and powerful. Symbols support overrides, nested symbols, and resizing rules. Sketch also added Variables in 2023 for color and text tokens. The Sketch Library lets you share symbols across files. Sketch's symbol system is slightly less flexible than Figma's variants, but for solo designers, it is more than sufficient. The main limitation is that Sketch Libraries are Mac-only, so sharing across a cross-platform team is harder.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best component flexibility goes to <span class="winner-tag">Figma</span>. Best native symbol maturity goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sketch</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing depends on team size and whether you need cross-platform collaboration. Sketch is cheaper for individuals, while Figma is better value for teams.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Figma</th><th>Sketch</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Free</td><td>3 projects, unlimited viewers</td><td>30-day trial</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Professional</td><td>$12/editor/mo</td><td>$10/editor/mo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Annual</td><td>$144/editor/yr</td><td>$120/editor/yr</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Organization</td><td>$45/editor/mo</td><td>Not available</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mac License</td><td>Not available</td><td>$120 one-time (standalone)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Unlimited Viewers</td><td>Yes, all plans</td><td>Yes, via Sketch Cloud</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dev Mode</td><td>Included</td><td>Yes, via Sketch Cloud</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FigJam / Whiteboard</td><td>Included</td><td>Not available</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Education Discount</td><td>Free for students</td><td>50% off</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Annual Billing Discount</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>For solo Mac designers, Sketch at $10 per month or $120 per year is cheaper than Figma Professional at $12 per month. The Sketch Mac license at $120 one-time is the best value for designers who do not need collaboration.</p>
    <p>For teams, Figma Professional at $12 per editor per month includes real-time collaboration, Dev Mode, and FigJam, which Sketch cannot fully match. The per-editor cost is higher, but the collaboration value justifies it for most teams.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best individual value goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sketch</span>. Best team value goes to <span class="winner-tag">Figma</span>.</p>

    <h2>Plugin Ecosystem and Integrations</h2>
    <p>Plugins extend what a design tool can do. Sketch has a larger ecosystem, while Figma's is growing faster.</p>

    <h3>Sketch Plugins</h3>
    <p>Sketch has thousands of plugins built over more than a decade, covering automation, export, accessibility, data population, and integration with tools like Jira, Zeplin, and Abstract. The Sketch plugin ecosystem is more mature than Figma's, and many plugins are deeply integrated with Mac-specific workflows. For designers who rely on plugins for productivity, Sketch is the stronger choice.</p>

    <h3>Figma Plugins</h3>
    <p>Figma launched official plugin support in 2019 and has grown rapidly, with hundreds of plugins covering accessibility, content population, export, animation, and integration with tools like Slack, Notion, and Jira. Figma plugins run in the browser, which makes them cross-platform, but they are generally less powerful than Sketch's native plugins. Figma also supports integrations through its API and the Figma Community, where designers share free resources.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best plugin maturity goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sketch</span>. Best cross-platform plugin support goes to <span class="winner-tag">Figma</span>.</p>

    <h2>Performance and Native Experience</h2>
    <p>How the tool feels day-to-day matters. Sketch has a native performance edge on Mac, while Figma is fast enough in the browser.</p>

    <h3>Sketch Performance</h3>
    <p>Sketch is a native Mac app, which means it launches instantly, handles large files smoothly, and uses macOS APIs for rendering. Scrolling, zooming, and selection feel snappier than browser-based tools. Sketch also works offline, which is valuable for designers who travel or work in areas with unreliable internet. For Mac purists, Sketch's native experience is a meaningful advantage.</p>

    <h3>Figma Performance</h3>
    <p>Figma runs in the browser, but its rendering engine is highly optimized. For most files, performance is indistinguishable from native apps. However, very large files with thousands of frames can feel sluggish, especially on older hardware. Figma's desktop app provides some offline caching, but true offline editing is limited. For most users, Figma's performance is fine, but native Mac users will notice the difference.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Sketch</span> wins native performance and offline experience.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>Figma Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Best real-time multiplayer collaboration in the category</li>
        <li>Cross-platform support including Windows and Linux</li>
        <li>Advanced prototyping with variables and conditional logic</li>
        <li>Dev Mode for developer handoff included</li>
        <li>FigJam whiteboard included at no extra cost</li>
        <li>Unlimited free viewers on all plans</li>
        <li>Browser-based, no installation required</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Figma Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>More expensive than Sketch at $12 per editor per month</li>
        <li>No true offline editing</li>
        <li>Large files can feel sluggish in browser</li>
        <li>Plugin ecosystem younger than Sketch</li>
        <li>No one-time license option</li>
        <li>Requires internet for most work</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Sketch Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Native Mac performance, fast and smooth</li>
        <li>Cheaper at $10 per editor per month</li>
        <li>One-time Mac license available at $120</li>
        <li>Full offline editing</li>
        <li>Mature and large plugin ecosystem</li>
        <li>Mature symbol and component system</li>
        <li>JSON-based file format for version control</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Sketch Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Mac-only, no Windows or Linux support</li>
        <li>Real-time collaboration weaker than Figma</li>
        <li>Prototyping less advanced than Figma</li>
        <li>No FigJam equivalent for whiteboarding</li>
        <li>Cloud collaboration requires Sketch Cloud subscription</li>
        <li>Market share declining vs Figma</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Cross-Platform Product Teams</h3>
      <p>If your team includes designers, product managers, and developers on Windows, Linux, or mixed hardware, Figma is the only practical choice. Its browser-based editing and real-time collaboration work across all operating systems, so every teammate can contribute. The $12 per editor per month cost is justified by the collaboration value.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Solo Mac Designers</h3>
      <p>If you are a solo designer working on a Mac and do not need real-time collaboration, Sketch is the better value. At $10 per month or $120 per year, it is cheaper than Figma, and the native Mac performance is noticeably better. The one-time Mac license at $120 is the best deal for designers who want to own their tool outright.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Remote and Hybrid Teams</h3>
      <p>If your team works remotely or in a hybrid setup, Figma is the better choice. Real-time multiplayer editing, live cursors, and built-in comments make async and sync collaboration seamless. Sketch Cloud works for async collaboration, but live co-editing is slower and less fluid than Figma.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>All-Mac Agencies</h3>
      <p>If your entire agency uses Macs and you value native performance and a mature plugin ecosystem, Sketch remains a strong choice. The JSON-based file format works well with version control systems like Git, and the plugin ecosystem is deeper than Figma's. For agencies that have invested in Sketch workflows, switching may not be worth the disruption.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Design System Teams</h3>
      <p>If you are building and maintaining a design system across multiple teams, Figma is the better choice. Its Variables, component properties, and Team Library make it easier to manage a shared design system at scale. Sketch's symbol system is mature, but sharing across teams is harder due to the Mac-only limitation.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Designing a mobile app with a remote team</h3>
      <p>A product team with designers in San Francisco, engineers in Berlin, and a product manager in Tokyo needs to design a mobile app. Figma lets all three work in the same file simultaneously, with live cursors and comments. The engineer inspects designs in Dev Mode, the PM leaves feedback on specific frames, and the designer iterates in real-time. Sketch would require the designer to upload files to Sketch Cloud for async feedback, losing the live collaboration.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Building a design system from scratch</h3>
      <p>A design lead needs to build a design system with color tokens, typography, and components. Figma's Variables let them define color and number tokens that can be applied across the system, and component variants make it easy to create button states, input variations, and icon sets. The Team Library shares the system across all projects. Sketch can do similar work with symbols and variables, but sharing across a cross-platform team is harder.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Prototyping a complex onboarding flow</h3>
      <p>A designer needs to prototype a 7-step onboarding flow with conditional logic, form validation, and animated transitions. Figma's Prototype mode with variables and smart animate handles the entire flow in one file, with no plugins required. The prototype can be shared via a link and tested on mobile devices. Sketch's native prototyping would require plugins for conditional logic, and the result would be less polished.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Designing offline during travel</h3>
      <p>A designer is traveling and needs to work on a design file during a long flight without internet. Sketch works fully offline as a native Mac app, so the designer can open, edit, and save files locally. Figma's desktop app has limited offline support, and the browser version requires internet, so the designer would need to plan ahead and cache files before traveling.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best for teams and cross-platform collaboration: Figma.</strong> Figma Professional at $12 per editor per month delivers the best real-time collaboration, cross-platform support, and integrated prototyping in the category. For product teams working remotely or across operating systems, Figma is the default choice and the reason it overtook Sketch as the market leader.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for solo Mac designers: Sketch.</strong> Sketch at $10 per editor per month or $120 per year offers native Mac performance, full offline editing, and a mature plugin ecosystem at a lower price than Figma. For solo designers or all-Mac agencies that do not need real-time collaboration, Sketch is the better value.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for prototyping and design systems: Figma.</strong> Figma's advanced prototyping with variables and conditional logic, combined with its component and Variables system, makes it the stronger tool for building interactive prototypes and scalable design systems.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended combo:</strong> For solo Mac designers who occasionally collaborate, use Sketch for daily design work at $10 per month, and use the free Figma tier for sharing and handoff. Total cost is $10 per month, and you get native Mac performance plus web-based collaboration when needed.</p>
    </div>

